{
  "dom_id": "step-q72188",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region that lies outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and inside the square defined by the lines $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=-1$ and $y=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14150788/assets/e762e510b690d65dfc8365d041c734d7b16611411004723a2abdbb74bfb41cc3.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14150788/assets/b72bb25ce1dcce3dc7792bbbcb8a878dde14df3a90c9243a3c75ac26a19aaa47.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14150788/assets/eb34633d98011ed7050694c722bc7c736a95939783efec3321d95517695642e2.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
